TI  - Honey Adulteration Detection Using Explainable XGBoost Classifier: A Physicochemical Feature-Based Approach
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Emerging Technologies and Innovation for Sustainability, EmergIN 2025
SP  - 832
EP  - 837
DO  - 10.1109/EmergIN67762.2025.11450830
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036372222?origin=resultslist
AD  - Srm Institute of Science and Technology, Department of Cse, Ramapuram, Chennai, India
AB  - Honey adulteration is a growing concern in the food industry, with direct implications for consumer health and market authenticity. This study proposes a robust machine learning framework enhanced with Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) to accurately detect adulterated honey samples and interpret model decisions. A curated dataset comprising 1,000 laboratory-verified honey samples - equally split between pure and adulterated variants - was utilized. Each sample was characterized by 12 physicochemical attributes, including pH, electrical conductivity, sugar profile (glucose, fructose, sucrose), moisture content, ash percentage, and hydroxymethylfurfural (HMF) levels. The data preparation involved imputing missing data, min-max scaling, and handling of abnormal data. In order to encourage generalization, feature selection was based on mutual information criteria and Recursive feature elimination. Three classifiers (Random Forest, Support Vector machine (SVM), and XGBoost) were trained and evaluated by use of 10-fold cross-validation. XGBoost performed the best with the highest accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and AUC of 97.4, 96.9, 97.2, 97.0, and 98.1 respectively. To enhance model transparency, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) was integrated post-classification, allowing interpretation of feature influence on individual predictions. SHAP results revealed that HMF, sucrose content, and moisture level were the most critical indicators influencing classification outcomes. The integration of XAI not only bolstered user trust but also enabled domain experts to validate model logic against known chemical patterns. Overall, the proposed framework offers a high-performing and interpretable solution for honey adulteration detection, suitable for deployment in food quality labs and regulatory environments. © 2025 IEEE.
